    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A bedroom should be the place where you can switch off. For many people, including autistic people, people with ADHD or sensory processing differences, and anyone who finds noise, bright light or busy rooms tiring, the way a bedroom is decorated can make it much easier or much harder to relax.</p>
<p>Sensory-friendly bedroom decor is about giving the person who uses the room more control over what they see, hear and feel. There is no single correct look. Some people are sensory-avoiding and want a quiet, muted room; others are sensory-seeking and feel calmer with deep pressure, movement or a favourite texture. Many people are a mix of both. The ideas below are a starting point, and the best guide is always the person themselves.</p>
<p>A note before you start: this is design advice, not therapy. Everyone's sensory needs are different. If you are planning a room for a child or adult with specific sensory needs, an occupational therapist can assess those needs and advise on what is likely to help.</p>

<h2 id="start-by-asking">Start by asking, observing and noting</h2>
<p>Before buying anything, spend a little time working out what the room needs to do and what makes it uncomfortable now.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>What feels too much?</strong> Bright overhead light, street noise, a humming fridge next door, itchy fabrics, strong patterns or a cluttered view from the bed.</li>
<li><strong>What helps?</strong> A weighted or heavy blanket, a particular soft toy, a dim corner, background sound, a certain colour.</li>
<li><strong>When is the room used?</strong> Sleeping only, or also for homework, play, gaming or quiet time?</li>
<li><strong>Involve the person.</strong> Where possible, let them choose colours, fabrics and the layout. Owning the decisions is part of what makes the room feel safe.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="calm-colour-palette">A calm, low-contrast colour palette</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-pendant-lights-cove-ceiling|A soft neutral palette, upholstered bed and sheer curtains give this bedroom a quiet, low-contrast look.-->
<p>Colour is one of the easiest things to change and has a big impact on how busy a room feels.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Choose muted, softer shades.</strong> Many people find greyed-down greens, soft blues, warm neutrals, oatmeal and dusty pastels easier to live with than bright, saturated colours.</li>
<li><strong>Keep contrast low.</strong> Stark combinations such as black and white, or a bright feature wall next to white woodwork, can feel visually loud. Painting walls, woodwork and even the ceiling in similar tones creates a gentler, enveloping effect.</li>
<li><strong>Go easy on pattern.</strong> Busy wallpapers, bold geometrics and stripes can be distracting. If the person loves pattern, keep it to something they can change easily, such as a cushion or a removable wall decal.</li>
<li><strong>Choose matt finishes.</strong> Matt or flat paint reduces glare and reflections compared with gloss or satin walls.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/bedroom-colour-guide">bedroom colour guide</a> covers specific shades and how room orientation changes them.</p>

<h2 id="reduce-visual-clutter">Reduce visual clutter</h2>
<p>A room full of objects, open shelves and things on the floor gives the eye no rest. Concealed storage is one of the most useful sensory-friendly changes you can make.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Closed storage over open shelves:</strong> wardrobes with doors, drawers, and lidded boxes in one colour keep things out of sight.</li>
<li><strong>Clear the view from the bed.</strong> Look at the room lying down. What you see last at night and first in the morning should be calm.</li>
<li><strong>Label clearly.</strong> Picture or word labels on drawers and boxes make it easier to find things and put them back, which helps keep the room tidy.</li>
<li><strong>Edit decorations.</strong> A few meaningful pieces feel calmer than lots of small ones. Rotate items rather than displaying everything at once.</li>
<li><strong>Hide cables and electronics</strong> where you can, or keep screens in a cupboard or a separate zone.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="lighting">Lighting: blackout, dimmable and no glare</h2>
<p>Light is often the biggest sensory issue in a bedroom. The aim is control: dark when it needs to be dark, soft when it needs to be soft.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blackout curtains or blinds.</strong> Blackout lining blocks streetlight and early morning sun. For the most darkness, choose a blackout blind that fits inside the window recess, layered with blackout-lined curtains that extend 15–20 cm (6–8 in) past each side of the frame to reduce light leaking around the edges.</li>
<li><strong>Dimmable lighting.</strong> Dimmer switches, plug-in dimmers or smart bulbs let the person set a level that feels right.</li>
<li><strong>Warm, indirect light.</strong> Warm-white bulbs (around 2700K), fabric or paper shades and lamps that bounce light off the walls are usually gentler than a bare bulb or a bright ceiling light.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid flicker and glare.</strong> Some people are sensitive to the flicker of certain cheap LED bulbs. If that is the case, try a few different bulbs and choose ones labelled flicker-free where available. Position lamps so the bulb is not visible from the bed.</li>
<li><strong>A gentle night light.</strong> A low, warm light for getting up in the night, rather than switching on the main light.</li>
<li><strong>Cover standby lights.</strong> Small LEDs on chargers, TVs and alarm clocks can be surprisingly bright in a dark room. Cover them or move the device.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/bedroom-lighting-and-curtains-guide">bedroom lighting and curtains guide</a> goes into more detail on curtain linings and lamp choices.</p>

<h2 id="soft-textures">Soft, comfortable textures</h2>
<!--figure:curved-upholstered-bed|A curved boucle bed, textured plaster wall and woven rattan pendant bring soft, tactile texture to this bedroom.-->
<p>Touch matters as much as sight. What feels lovely to one person can feel scratchy or irritating to another, so try fabrics in person before buying.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bedding:</strong> many people prefer smooth, breathable fabrics such as cotton sateen, cotton jersey, bamboo-derived fabrics or washed linen. Remove scratchy labels.</li>
<li><strong>Weighted or heavier blankets:</strong> some people find the pressure comforting. Weighted blankets are not suitable for everyone, particularly young children; follow the manufacturer's weight and age guidance and ask an occupational therapist if you are unsure.</li>
<li><strong>Rugs:</strong> a soft rug beside the bed means a warm, pleasant surface first thing in the morning.</li>
<li><strong>Fidget and comfort textures:</strong> for sensory seekers, a velvet cushion, a faux fur throw or a textured wall hanging can give the hands something to explore.</li>
<li><strong>Upholstered furniture:</strong> rounded, padded headboards and furniture without sharp corners feel softer, both to touch and to look at.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="sound-softening">Softening sound</h2>
<p>Hard floors, bare walls and uncovered windows make a room echo. Soft furnishings absorb sound and make a bedroom feel quieter.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rugs and underlays:</strong> a large rug on a hard floor reduces echo and footstep noise.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy curtains:</strong> thick, lined or interlined curtains help soften sound from outside as well as blocking light.</li>
<li><strong>Upholstered pieces:</strong> a fabric headboard, an armchair and plenty of cushions all absorb sound.</li>
<li><strong>Bookshelves and fabric wall hangings</strong> on a shared wall can help soften sound a little.</li>
<li><strong>Fix the small noises:</strong> oil a squeaky door hinge, add felt pads to furniture legs, and move a ticking clock.</li>
<li><strong>Background sound:</strong> some people find a fan or a white noise machine helpful; others need silence. Ear defenders or earplugs can be kept in a set place for when they are needed.</li>
</ul>
<p>Decor softens sound but will not soundproof a room. For significant noise problems, speak to an acoustic specialist.</p>

<h2 id="defined-zones">Create defined zones</h2>
<p>When a bedroom has to do several jobs, giving each activity its own clear area helps the brain know what the space is for.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sleep zone:</strong> the bed, kept as calm as possible, with minimal screens or toys.</li>
<li><strong>Calm-down or retreat zone:</strong> a small, enclosed space such as a canopy, a pop-up tent, a reading nook with floor cushions or a bean bag in a corner. Being partly enclosed can feel very comforting.</li>
<li><strong>Activity zone:</strong> a desk or play area, ideally facing a plain wall rather than a window or busy shelf.</li>
<li><strong>Use rugs, lighting and furniture</strong> to mark the edges of each zone, so the boundaries are clear even in a small room.</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on layouts, see our <a href="/blog/bedroom-furniture-arrangement-guide">bedroom furniture arrangement guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="personal-comfort">Personal comfort items</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep favourite comfort objects, such as a particular blanket, soft toy or photograph, in a consistent, easy-to-reach place.</li>
<li>Make temperature easy to control with layered bedding rather than one heavy duvet, and a fan or an extra blanket within reach.</li>
<li>Choose fragrance carefully. Many people prefer an unscented room; if scent is welcome, keep it very light and avoid anything that cannot be switched off.</li>
<li>Keep the room predictable. Once it works, avoid frequent rearranging unless the person wants change.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="safety">Safety notes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Anchor tall furniture to the wall, especially in children's rooms.</li>
<li>Keep blind cords out of reach and use cord tidies or cordless blinds.</li>
<li>Check that soft furnishings meet fire-safety labelling requirements.</li>
<li>For any specialist equipment, such as weighted blankets, swings or bed tents, follow the manufacturer's guidance and seek professional advice.</li>
</ul>

